-- title:
--   Classifying preimage of j(mathsf q^{qd}) computing quotient j-invariants
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell_g$ a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$.
--
--   *Reduction data at $q$.* A valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` is given with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ lies in the nonunits of $A$. A finite set $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` (the subfield of `LaurentSeries (ResidueField A)` generated over the residue field of $A$ by `jqModC` and `jqNModC … M'`) over that residue field is given, and `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the places in `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, i.e. of those places satisfying the predicate `IsSupersingularPlace q M'`; $s$ is a chosen element of $W$. The hypothesis `hle` requires the intermediate field `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` — the subfield of `LaurentSeries (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)` obtained by adjoining to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ the coefficientwise images of the full level-$M'$ modular function field — to be contained in `fieldBar q M'`, which is `xHFunctionFieldBar (q^2*M') (levelH q M')`, where `levelH q M'` is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$. Furthermore $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` to `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`: a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of the former, a surjective ring homomorphism `R₀.residue` onto the latter whose kernel is the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, together with the compatibility of membership and of residues with $A$, the existence for each nonzero element of a scalar making it an integer with nonzero residue, and the compatibility of the pushforward of divisors with orders of residues. The hypothesis `hR₀` says that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in `LaurentSeries (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)` lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that element lies in `R₀.integers` and its residue, read inside `LaurentSeries (ResidueField A)`, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$ along the residue map of $A$.
--
--   *The local base ring.* An intermediate field $k_0$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ is given together with $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lying in $A$; write $A_0 :=$ `A.comap (algebraMap k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))` for the induced valuation subring of $k_0$. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` require $A_0$ to be a discrete valuation ring whose maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$, to be a henselian local ring, and to have algebraically closed residue field; `hκ` requires every $a \in A$ to be congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   *Roots of unity, level and function field.* An element $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $(q\ell_g)$-th root of unity (`hξ`), and `hιξ` asks for a ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell_g))$. The subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ is required by `hH₁` to be the intersection of `levelH q M'` with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$; thus $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell_g$. The intermediate field $K$ of `LaurentSeries k₀` over $k_0$ is required by `hK` to be `laurentBaseChange k₀ (xHFunctionField (q^2*M') H₁)`, the field generated over $k_0$ by the coefficientwise images of the $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$ function field over $\mathbb Q$; $K$ is an $A_0$-algebra compatibly with $k_0$. Finally $j \in K$ is nonzero and, as a Laurent series, equals `coeffEmb k₀ jq`, the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-function pushed to $k_0$.
--
--   *Functoriality input for the moduli problem.* The hypotheses `hℓ`, `hM`, `hL` require, for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every variable change $C$: that `IsGamma1Point W ℓg D` implies `IsGamma1Point (C • W) ℓg (D.variableChange C)` for level-$P$ data $D$; that `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` implies `IsGamma0PowAt (C • W) p k (kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h)`; and that $h \mid$ `inLineMulPoly W ℓg n x` implies `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` $\mid$ `inLineMulPoly (C • W) ℓg n (u^{-2}(x - C.r))`. A family of group laws $\mathcal G$ on `GroupLaws A₀` is given with `h𝒢 : 𝒢.IsChordTangent` and `h𝒢O : 𝒢.IsOriginIdentity`, and a level transport $\mathcal T$ on `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q` with `h𝒯 : 𝒯.IsSectionTransport`. The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` provide, for every $A_0$-algebra $T$ and projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, graded ring homomorphisms between the graded rings `projModelGradingCR` which satisfy `IsVariableChangeHom` for a variable change $C$, respectively `IsCoefficientHom` along an $A_0$-algebra map $f$, each together with the requirement that the irrelevant ideal of the target be contained in the image of the irrelevant ideal of the source.
--
--   Write $D :=$ `rigidDataH1Pow A₀ ℓg M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯` for the resulting rigid Weierstrass data, whose raw points over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ consist of a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant, a $\Gamma_0(p^{v_p(M')})$-kernel polynomial for each prime factor $p$ of $M'$, a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point datum, and a Drinfeld $q$-level pair of sections, subject to the $\Gamma_1$-link condition.
--
--   *The universal object and the Tate point.* $P_0$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for $D$`.toLevelModuliDatum`: an $A_0$-algebra $B_0 :=$ `P₀.B₀` with a point `P₀.univ` over $B_0$ such that every point over any $A_0$-algebra $T$ is the image of `P₀.univ` under a unique $A_0$-algebra map $B_0 \to T$. A point $x_0$ of $D$`.toLevelModuliDatum` over $K$ is given with `hx₀`: its $j$-invariant `jOf x₀`, read in `LaurentSeries k₀`, is `jqNModC k₀ q`, the image of `jqModC` under the $q$-expansion substitution of index $q$. The $A_0$-algebra map `clC` goes from $B_0$ to the chart algebra `TwoChartIntegralModel.chartAlgFin A₀ K j`, and `hclC` requires its composite with the inclusion into $K$ to be the classifying map `P₀.classify x₀`.
--
--   The hypothesis `hμ` (six clauses) asserts the existence of a variable change $C$ over `LaurentSeries k₀` and a raw point $r$ of $D$ over $K$ such that: the class of $r$ in $D$`.toLevelModuliDatum.Pt K` is $x_0$; writing $(x_\ast,y_\ast) :=$ `tateToricPoint k₀ q (ξ^q)` (the unit $\xi$ raised to the $q$-th power), one has $u\,(2x_\ast + \mathrm{C}(1/6)) = 2y_\ast + x_\ast$ for $u = C.u$, together with $C.r = \mathrm{C}(-1/12)$, $C.s = \mathrm{C}(-1/2)$, $C.t = \mathrm{C}(1/24)$ for the constant Hahn series; the curve of $r$, base changed to `LaurentSeries k₀`, equals $C \bullet$ `tateBase k₀ q`; for every prime factor $p$ of $M'$, every field $F'$, every ring homomorphism $f : k_0 \to F'$ and every primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta$ in $F'$, the polynomial $r$`.level.1 p`, base changed to `LaurentSeries k₀` and then pushed along `coeffMap f`, equals `kernelVariableChangeDeg (C.map (coeffMap f)) (gamma0PowDeg p (M'.factorization p))` applied to $\prod_a (X - \mathrm{C}((\mathrm{toricPoint}\ F'\,q\,(\zeta^a)).1))$, the product over $a \in [1, p^{v_p(M')}/2]$ with $p \nmid a$; the level-$P$ datum $r$`.level.2.1`, base changed to `LaurentSeries k₀`, equals the variable change by $C$ of the datum $\langle x_\ast, y_\ast, x_\ast, y_\ast\rangle$; and there exist $P_x, P_y, Q_x, Q_y \in K$ whose images in `LaurentSeries k₀` are the four coordinates `xP`, `yP`, `xQ`, `yQ` of $($`cuspData k₀ q (ξ^{ℓg}) ![1,0] ![0,-1]`$)$`.variableChange C`, such that `IsSectionThrough r.level.2.2.P Px Py` and `IsSectionThrough r.level.2.2.Q Qx Qy` hold, i.e. the two Drinfeld sections are cut out in the $Z$-chart by these affine coordinates.
--
--   *Conclusion.* For every nonzero $d$ dividing $M'$ such that `jqNModC k₀ (q*d)` lies in $K$ (`hcK`) and the corresponding element of $K$ lies in the chart algebra `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` (`hcC`), there exists $c \in B_0$ with the following two properties.
--
--   First, `clC c` is that element of the chart algebra, namely $\langle\langle$`jqNModC k₀ (q*d)`$, h_{cK}\rangle, h_{cC}\rangle$.
--
--   Second, for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ that is an $A_0$-algebra, every $A_0$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi : B_0 \to \Omega$ which annihilates the kernel of `clC` (that is, $\varphi b' = 0$ whenever `clC b' = 0`), with $M'$ invertible in the sense that $(M' : \Omega) \ne 0$, every raw point $x_\Omega$ of $D$ over $\Omega$ whose class in $D$`.Pt Ω` is the image of `P₀.univ` under $\varphi$, and every point $g$ of the affine curve of $x_\Omega$`.curve` satisfying the two conditions of `hg` — that $g$ has exact additive order $M'$, and that for every prime factor $p$ of $M'$, every $n$ and every nonsingular affine point $(x_1,y_1)$ with $n \cdot g = (x_1,y_1)$ and $n \cdot g$ of exact order $p^{v_p(M')}$, the polynomial $x_\Omega$`.level.1 p` has $x_1$ as a root — one has
--   $$\varphi(c) = \mathrm{cyclicQuotientJ}\ (x_\Omega.\text{curve})\ \big(\mathrm{AddSubgroup.zmultiples}\ ((M'/d) \cdot g)\big)\ d,$$
--   that is, $\varphi(c)$ is the quantity $c_4^3/\Delta$ of the curve `cyclicQuotientCurve` attached to $x_\Omega$`.curve`, the cyclic subgroup generated by $(M'/d)\cdot g$, and the parameter $d$.
--
--   This is the $H_1$-level, étale-companion form of the statement that the function $j(\mathsf q^{qd})$ on the $j$-finite chart of the two-chart integral model of $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$ comes from an element of the universal ring $B_0$ of the moduli package, and that this element reads off, at every geometric point of the chart, the $j$-invariant of the quotient of the classified curve by the cyclic subgroup of order $d$ inside the chosen $M'$-torsion point. It is the glue of three earlier results — surjectivity of the chart-algebra classifying map onto the chart value, the identification of $j(\mathsf q^{qd})$ with the quotient $j$-invariant over $K$, and the transfer of that identity to arbitrary algebraically closed fibres — and it is used downstream in the analysis of level automorphisms of the same moduli package.
